scChromatic third-party palette notice
======================================

This file documents provenance; it is not legal advice and does not claim that
the package-level GPL license automatically resolves every upstream palette's
copyright or licensing terms. Entries marked provenance_review in
palette-provenance.csv should be reviewed before redistribution in contexts
with additional licensing requirements.

ggsci
-----

The package organization and scales-compatible interface were informed by
ggsci (https://github.com/nanxstats/ggsci), Copyright Nan Xiao and
contributors, licensed GPL (>= 3). scChromatic reimplements its interfaces for
persistent single-cell color systems; no ggsci palette vectors are included.

ArchR
-----

The archr_* vectors are frozen from GreenleafLab/ArchR, R/ColorPalettes.R,
commit 6feec354ad6c8052ddbc4626a2ca2d858ed465bf:
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GreenleafLab/ArchR/6feec354ad6c8052ddbc4626a2ca2d858ed465bf/R/ColorPalettes.R

ArchR is distributed under the MIT License. ArchR explicitly describes
ArchRPalettes as a collection containing both original and borrowed palettes.
Accordingly, scChromatic does not claim that every archr_* palette was designed
by ArchR. Source comments identify zissou, darjeeling, and rushmore with
wesanderson; sambaNight and solarExtra with buencolors. The ColorBrewer-like
whitePurple, whiteBlue, and greenBlue sequences retain provenance_review status.

Okabe-Ito
----------

The Okabe-Ito color universal design palette is documented by Masataka Okabe
and Kei Ito at https://jfly.uni-koeln.de/color/. The fixed hexadecimal values
are reproduced with attribution.

Paul Tol
--------

Tol bright, vibrant, muted, and medium-contrast schemes are from Paul Tol,
"Colour Schemes", https://personal.sron.nl/~pault/. The source permits free
personal and commercial use with attribution; consult the current source terms.

Glasbey
-------

The Glasbey palette is attributed to Glasbey CA, van der Heijden GWAM, Toh VFK,
and Gray AJ (2007), "Colour Displays for Categorical Images", Color Research
and Application 32:304-309, https://doi.org/10.1002/col.20327. The registry
retains provenance_review status for the distributed hexadecimal transcription.

Polychrome
----------

The 36-color palette is attributed to the Polychrome R package and Coombes KR,
Brock G, Abrams ZB, and Abruzzo LV (2019), "Polychrome: Creating and Assessing
Qualitative Palettes with Many Colors", Journal of Statistical Software 90,
Code Snippet 1, https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v090.c01. Polychrome is GPL-2.

dittoSeq
--------

ditto40 reproduces the fixed colors identified in dittoSeq's dittoColors()
source as of dittoSeq v0.2.10:
https://github.com/dtm2451/dittoSeq/blob/master/R/dittoColors.R

dittoSeq attributes its base colors to Wong's color-vision-deficiency-aware
palette. Only entries 1-7 are described as the strongest red-green
color-vision-deficiency-aware subset. Entries 8-40 are grey plus lighter and
darker extensions and are not described here as fully color-blind safe.

viridisLite
-----------

viridis, cividis, and magma anchors are generated at package-data build time
with viridisLite (MIT), https://sjmgarnier.github.io/viridisLite/. The original
matplotlib colormaps are by Nathaniel Smith, Stefan van der Walt, and Eric
Firing; cividis has separate scientific-map attribution documented upstream.

scico
-----

scico_batlow, scico_lajolla, scico_vik, and scico_broc are interoperability
registry entries. Their values are provided only at runtime by the optional
scico package (MIT), https://github.com/thomasp85/scico, using Fabio Crameri's
Scientific colour maps. Consult the Scientific colour maps terms and cite
Crameri, Shephard, and Heron (2020), Nature Communications 11:5444.

Generated scChromatic palettes
------------------------------

chromatic is generated for scChromatic and frozen as a nested, deterministic
40-color sequence under GPL (>= 3). Runtime qualitative extension also belongs
to scChromatic. These colors are perceptually optimized and audited against
normal, deutan, protan, and tritan simulations; they are not claimed to be
universally color-blind safe.
